ukraine and russia have agreed on  humanitarian quarters to evacuate  thousands of civilians eastern regions  of ukraine are bracing for a new russian  offensive there more than a hundred  thousand people are believed trapped in  the city of maria paul which has been  under intense bombardment for weeks  local officials have said as many as 5  000 civilians may have been killed in  that city ukrainians are fleeing the  eastern donbass region which has been  partially controlled  by russian-backed separatists since  2014.  now satellite images show a convoy of  hundreds of russian military vehicles  heading for the donbus region and that's  making the evacuation of civilians all  the more urgent  and our correspondent rebecca ritters  has seen what some of that evacuation  looks like rebecca thanks for joining us  what is first of all the latest from  the luhans that region that's part of  donbass in the east  well we are starting to see come to  fruition what we've been expecting the  regrouping of russian troops and the the  pivot towards to focus on the south east  of the of ukraine  and the the south and the east  including luhansk and donetsk as you  just mentioned uh luhansk it's it's  prompted a  more  a stronger effort to get people out of  there luhan's governor has  basically told everyone to get out while  they still can um unfortunately not  everyone is heeding that warning there  is still apparently 30 percent of people  staying in lahars i actually met a guy  who was reporting down in the region  close to near there and i spoke to a  gentleman at the train station and he he  broke down in tears as he was describing  to me uh that some of his friends from  the luhansk and yes region were actually  choosing to stay they'd already lost  everything in the battle in 2014 and  they weren't going to leave again they  said if the russians come in they're  just going to fight them and he he was  really obviously very upset i mean  everyone has seen the pictures from  mariupol as you just mentioned and uh  also around kiev in butcher and nobody  wants those kind of atrocities happening  to their towns and villages william  rebecca you're also in nipro um another  city it's in central eastern uh ukraine  and it's become something of a hub for  internally displaced ukrainians what did  you see there  well nepro is a transport hub in that  region it services the south and the  south east we were at the train station  doing some reporting yesterday and we  saw thousands of people evacuating  thousands of people have already come  through there and they're expecting  thousands more the situation of course  was pretty tense yesterday because it  was just shortly after a day after the  um that bombing at the train station in  kramatorsk which was just 200 or so  kilometers from nepal so authorities  were on really high alert there  yesterday really worried that a similar  attack might take place there a lot of  the people i spoke to just work had come  from the the regions that we're speaking  about those lehigh donets regions but  they just had no idea where they were  going to end up there is a shelter there  people are providing support for the  people that come through there but  they're not really able to stay in nepro  either the mayor of nepro has ordered  all women and children to leave the area  because they're worried that the  fighting is going to also reach that  city  it's difficult to imagine just what  people are facing as they're trying to  find some semblance of safety in ukraine  and you saw some of that traveling back  to la viv where you are now by train  which a lot of people are using  what kind of experience did you have  what what kind of people did you come  across on that journey  well it was an incredibly bittersweet  experience really i mean you see  a relief in some faces but on the other  side you see the sadness they're having  to leave people behind you so we saw you  know people we were on the train with  waving to their loved ones on the  platform who were both relieved that  their relatives were getting to safety  but obviously not knowing when or if  they're going to see them again so  obviously uh you know there was a lot of  sadness on the train the journey itself  was relatively normal i mean ukraine  railways have been doing an amazing job  at keeping this country going the  civilian effort both by getting aid into  places that desperately need it as well  as bringing people out of those hotspot  areas  there were a couple of amendments if you  will the blinds needed to be closed for  the entire journey at night when as soon  as the sun went down all the blinds had  to be closed so that you're less visible  from the sky  and the train also travels at a much  slower pace it took almost twice as long  as the normal journey because if  anything does  happen you know there's any kind of  shelling or anything like that the train  needs to be able to stop to evacuate the  passengers  incredible details there rebecca thank  you so much rebecca rebecca ritter  speaking to us from lev in western  ukraine  and let's get some more developments in  the war there  austrian chancellor khan emma is to meet  the russian leader vladimir putin in  moscow he'll be the first european  leader to do so since the invasion of  ukraine nehamer has been in kiev where  he offered humanitarian aid and  political support  a grave containing at least two  civilians has been uncovered in busova  village near kiev it's the latest burial  site identified since russian forces  withdrew from the area to concentrate on  eastern ukraine  the airport in nepal ukraine's fourth  biggest city has been badly damaged by  russian missile attacks that's according  to the local governor  thick columns of smoke could be seen  rising around the airport  you
 

